  "content": {
    "id": "p28-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "Show that the graph of $y=x^3-ax^2+x-a$ has exactly one $x$-intercept.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Exactly one $x$-intercept, at $(a,0)$.\n\nFactorising gives $y=(x-a)(x^2+1)$, and $x^2+1>0$ for every real $x$.",
      "worked": "Factorising in pairs gives\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=x^3-ax^2+x-a\\\\y&=x^2(x-a)+(x-a)\\\\y&=(x-a)(x^2+1).\\end{align*}$$\nFor every real $x$, $x^2+1>0$. Therefore, $y=0$ only when $x=a$, so the graph has exactly one $x$-intercept, $(a,0)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 36
  },
